the woman who delivered a powerful  statement about her sexual assault by a  Stanford student and gave voice to  survivors nationwide is now coming  forward previously known only as Emily  doe Chanel Miller is sharing her story  with 60 minutes correspondent Bill  Whitaker she's written a memoir set to  be published later this month  it expands on her victim impact  statement that was viewed 11 million  times in just four days after it was  posted online many who shared the letter  said her words inspired them to come  forward with their own stories you  cannot give me back the life I had three  years after her words became a national  rallying cry for survivors of sexual  assault  Janelle Miller is showing her face for  the first time 60 minutes recorded her  reading her 12th page impact statement  where she spoke of the importance of  reclaiming her life I had to force  myself to relearn my real name my  identity to relearn that this is not all  that I am Miller first read these words  back in 2016 at a sentencing hearing for  her assailant Brock Turner he was found  guilty of three counts of felony sexual  assault  Turner attacked Miller behind a dumpster  at a college frat party while she was  passed out two good Samaritans  intervened in newspapers my name was  unconscious intoxicated woman ten  syllables and nothing more than that  many were angered after Judge Aaron  Persky sentenced Turner to just six  months in county jail when he could have  faced up to 14 years behind bars  millions read Miller's powerful words  about her assault it was a pivotal  moment it focused our attention not only  on the devastation of sexual violence  but on the difficulties of pursuing a  case through the criminal justice system  her statement also led California to  pass minimum sentencing requirements for  sexual assault cases and last year  voters recalled Persky  the first such action in more than 80  years she gave voice to the feelings of  shame  an isolation that so many survivors of  sexual violence experienced now speaking  with her name is is one further step  toward her healing yeah you know in that  original impact statement she said that  that judge lit a tiny fire inside of her  because she wanted other people to feel  empowered you can tell that statement  and the way she has taken ownership of  this idea of I will control the  narrative I will speak for my name  exactly well you can see Bill Whitaker's  full interview with Chanel Miller Sunday  September 22nd on 60 minutes then Miller  will join us here on CBS this morning on  September 24th the same day her book no  my name will be released
 

